1. e4 Nf6 $6 { An
attacking player's reaction to this move should be: I can now turn this defender
of black's king into a nag. } { [%c_effect f6;square;f6;type;Inaccuracy;persistent;true] } 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. Nf3 $1 { Strategy. We tell black that our e5
pawn is the tip of our spear and it's not going away. We also say that the
kingside is our side and black's force count is low there. If they take we
intend to recapture with the knight. Making it easy. We just need to understand
the couple tries black has to undermine our e5 strongpoint. Style. We DO NOT
play for space with c4 and f4. We are playing for mate. } { [%c_effect f3;square;f3;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 4... dxe5 (4... Bg4 { The second we see this move we should think: \"light squares\". We'll see
several variations where black will be missing their bishop. } 5. Be2 e6 { A
secret of the opening: this e6 move is not just about developing their pieces,
black has to worry over us getting e6 in $1 } (5... Nc6 6. c4 Nb6 7. e6 $1 { A familiar theme from
our system against the Caro. It's not just that black's king is weak but that
it's difficult to develop their kingside. } { [%c_effect e6;square;e6;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 7... fxe6 (7... Bxe6 $2 { [%c_effect e6;square;e6;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 8. d5 { whoops }) 8. O-O { all of
black's pieces terrible are and he's going to get mated } 8... g6 9. Nbd2 $1 { We will win the light
squares with h3 and quickly bring our sad b1 knight into g5. } { [%c_effect d2;square;d2;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 9... Bg7 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Nxf3 O-O 12. d5 exd5 13. cxd5 Nb4 14. Ng5 $16 { We gain an octopus on e6
and the light squares are our freeway to the black king. }) (5... Nd7 6. c4 N5b6 7. Ng5 $1 { Note that Ng5
doesn't lose a tempo: when black takes on e2 they develop our queen. } { [%c_effect g5;square;g5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 7... Bxe2 8. Qxe2 e6 9. Qf3 Qe7 10. Qxb7 { We have the pawn and the compensation. Note that
by taking the pawn we've destroyed the light squares on black's queenside. } 10... dxe5 $2 { [%c_effect e5;square;e5;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 11. c5 $18 { Is the tactical point, black will lose their entire queenside. }) (5... g6 6. Ng5 { A key idea of ours against Bg4. Note that even though we are moving a
developed piece twice the move actually gains a tempo: black gives us one when
we get Qxe2 for free and has to play e6 because our own threat of e6 is too
powerful. } 6... Bxe2 7. Qxe2 e6 8. Qf3 Qd7 9. c4 Nb6 10. Ne4 Bg7 11. Nf6+ $18 { So
much pain on the dark squares. }) 6. O-O Be7 (6... dxe5 7. Nxe5 $1 { This recapture is at the heart of
our setup. } { [%c_effect e5;square;e5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 7... Bxe2 8. Qxe2 $16 { White's position is a dream, note that white
won a key tempo when black traded on e2. Note as well that we have a very
similar position vs the Scandi. }) 7. h3 { SPARRING POSITI $146 } 7... Bh5 { If black
just goes back then we claim that h3 was a useful move for us. } (7... Bxf3 8. Bxf3 { We saw this dynamic in the Scandi too, we play to open the position for
our unopposed light square bishop. } 8... Nc6 9. exd6 Qxd6 10. c3 { We put our
pawn on the opposite color of our unopposed bishop on f3 so we can control all
the squares. } 10... O-O 11. Nd2 $16 { We will strive to attack on the light squares
where black is down a piece. }) 8. c4 Nb6 9. Nc3 dxe5 (9... O-O 10. Be3 { It's
hard for black to move. Note that Nb6 is terrible and black's Q doesn't have a
natural square. } 10... d5 (10... Nc6 11. exd6 cxd6 (11... Bxd6 $2 { [%c_effect d6;square;d6;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 12. c5 $18 { Welcome to the dojo }) 12. d5 $16 { Black has to compromise their position, Be3 is a god, and Nb6 should make
black question ever playing the Alekhine's in the first place. }) 11. c5 Bxf3 12. Bxf3 Nc4 13. Bf4 $16 { Black's pieces are miserable and all we have to know is that
they can't take the pawn. } 13... Nxb2 $2 { [%c_effect b2;square;b2;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 14. Qb1 Nc4 15. Qxb7 Nd7 16. Nxd5 $1 { Black's light squares
collapse. } { [%c_effect d5;square;d5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 16... exd5 17. Qxd5 Na5 $18 (17... Na3 18. Qb3 { We get our piece
back. }) 18. c6 $18 { We get our piece back. }) 10. Nxe5 { We always want this
recapture. } 10... Bxe2 11. Qxe2 $1 $16 { Again: this trade won us a tempo.
We only need to know that black will pay for taking our d-pawn. If black doesn't
take then Rd1 comes and all of our pieces are great and Nb6 will tell its owner
to never play the Alekhine again. } { [%c_effect e2;square;e2;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 11... Qxd4 $2 { This leads to a mountain of pain for
black } { [%c_effect d4;square;d4;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 12. Rd1 Qc5 13. b4 $1 { [%c_effect b4;square;b4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 13... Qxb4 14. Nb5 $18 { And
black has two problems that can't be solved with one move: Nxc7 and Ba3. }) (4... g6 5. Bc4 { Note that c4 is not our move. c4 plays for space, we are playing for
mate. } 5... Nb6 6. Bb3 Bg7 7. Ng5 $1 { SPARRING POSITI $146 } { [%c_effect g5;square;g5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 7... e6 (7... O-O 8. e6 { A dream attack on the light squares. }) (7... d5 8. f4 O-O 9. O-O { Black's king is like a lonsome dude in a shark cage, and they'll have to open
the escapse hatch with f6 or h6 if they want our knight on g5 to leave. }) 8. Qf3 O-O 9. Qh3 $1 { Key move. } { [%c_effect h3;square;h3;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 9... h6 10. Nf3 { With a tremendous attack. We are obliged to do a death dance
every time our Q can park herself on our strong side. }) (4... Nd7 5. c4 Nb4 6. e6 $1 { Hello, old friend. } { [%c_effect e6;square;e6;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 6... fxe6 7. Nc3 { We've seen this party before. Black has to solve: Bc8, Bf8,
Qd8, Ra8 and Rh8 before the king gets mated. } 7... g6 8. Ng5 Nb6 9. a3 Nc6 10. d5 exd5 11. cxd5 Ne5 12. f4 Nf7 13. Bb5+ { I'll take the light squares for 400,
Bob. } 13... Bd7 14. Ne6 $18 { that hurts }) (4... Nc6 5. c4 Nb6 6. e6 $1 { Hello, old friend. } { [%c_effect e6;square;e6;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 6... fxe6 (6... Bxe6 $2 { [%c_effect e6;square;e6;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 7. d5 $18) 7. Nc3 { With a great attack, all of black's minors are terrible and their king doesn't
have a home. } 7... g6 8. h4 { hello friend } 8... Bg7 9. h5 { black is going to
get mated }) 5. Nxe5 Nd7 { I give this as the main line as challengin our strong
knight seems the only sensible way to play. Unfortunately for black there's a
problem. } (5... g6 6. Bc4 $1 { We have a hidden threat. } { [%c_effect c4;square;c4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 6... c6 { Always celebrate when black loses a tempo. } (6... Bg7 $2 { [%c_effect g7;square;g7;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 7. Nxf7 $1 { [%c_effect f7;square;f7;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 7... Kxf7 8. Qf3+ Ke6 9. Qe4+ $18 { We regain the piece with interest. }) 7. O-O Bg7 8. Re1 { ez chess }) (5... c6 { Obviously a sad move, black would like to play Nd7 immediately, but we see what
that gets him in the main line. } 6. Nc3 { white has several moves here, most play
for space, this one plays for mate } 6... Nd7 (6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 Nd7 8. Nf3 $1 { No trade for you $1 We
will build our familiar formation with Bd3, 0-0 and Re1. } { [%c_effect f3;square;f3;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] }) 7. Nxd5 cxd5 (7... Nxe5 8. dxe5 Qxd5 9. Qxd5 cxd5 10. Be3 { With a pleasant endgame (because we are
up in time) where we will continue to attack. Our king will go to d2. }) 8. Bd3 Nxe5 9. dxe5 { With a formation familiar to us from our Caro and French
systems. }) 6. Nxf7 $1 { Time to take the king for a walk } { [%c_effect f7;square;f7;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 6... Kxf7 7. Qh5+ Ke6 { in the pre-computer era
many pre-arranged draws saw Qg4, Kf7, Qh5, Ke6 } 8. c4 $1 { SPARRING POSITI $146 Attacking players
should use this as a training position, it's not just about learning an opening
line $1 } { [%c_effect c4;square;c4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 8... N5f6 (8... Nb4 9. a3 $1 { Mate wins the game. } { [%c_effect a3;square;a3;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 9... Nc2+ 10. Kd1 Nxa1 11. Qd5+ Kf6 12. Bg5+ Kg6 13. Bd3+ Kh5 14. g4+ Kxg4 15. Qf5+ Kh5 16. Be2#) 9. d5+ Kd6 10. Qf7 $1 { key move } { [%c_effect f7;square;f7;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 10... Ne5 11. Bf4 { pin
and win } 11... g5 { as Nc3 and 0-0-0 are coming black has to challenge the pin
